1
00:00:14,170 --> 00:00:20,610
2.1 Enterprise Environmental Factors
Enterprise Environmental Factors,

2
00:00:20,750 --> 00:00:26,530
EEFs, refer to conditions not under the
control of the project team that

3
00:00:26,531 --> 00:00:29,030
influence, constrain or direct the
project.

4
00:00:29,930 --> 00:00:33,610
These conditions can be internal and or
external to the organization.

5
00:00:34,310 --> 00:00:39,210
Enterprise environmental factors are
considered as inputs to many project

6
00:00:39,211 --> 00:00:43,130
management processes, specifically for
most planning processes.

7
00:00:43,131 --> 00:00:47,290
These factors may enhance our constrained
project management options.

8
00:00:48,030 --> 00:00:50,010
In addition, these factors may have a

9
00:00:50,011 --> 00:00:52,510
positive or negative
influence on the outcome.

10
00:00:52,950 --> 00:00:55,250
That's from PIMBOC guide, page 38.

11
00:00:55,370 --> 00:00:57,710
So what are these environmental factors
then?

12
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:01,630
There are many different types of
enterprise environmental factors,

13
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:05,590
but they can be divided into
two distinct groups, those that

14
00:01:05,591 --> 00:01:09,330
are external to the organization
and those that are internal.

15
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:14,530
I like to think of enterprise environmental
environmental factors this way.

16
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:17,441
Imagine you and your project team are

17
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:20,570
traveling to another
country on a cruise ship.

18
00:01:20,930 --> 00:01:23,030
This is where you're managing your
project.

19
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:30,170
The ship represents the organizational
enterprise environmental factors internal.

20
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:35,750
So the ones internal to the ship would be
resource availability, e.g.

21
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:41,590
the vision, mission, values, belief of the
shipping company, the rules on board the

22
00:01:41,591 --> 00:01:47,270
ship, the expected behaviour of the crew
and passengers, the Code of Ethics and

23
00:01:47,271 --> 00:01:50,366
Rule of Law, the leadership
of the captain, as well as

24
00:01:50,367 --> 00:01:54,390
the hierarchy and authority
relationships of crew members.

25
00:01:55,770 --> 00:02:00,730
Geographical distribution of facilities
and resources, e.g., there would be a

26
00:02:00,731 --> 00:02:05,570
floor plan of a ship showing the
facilities on each deck and you would have

27
00:02:05,571 --> 00:02:09,110
information about your cabins and where to
eat, for example.

28
00:02:10,310 --> 00:02:13,531
Infrastructure, e.g., there
would be details of what

29
00:02:13,532 --> 00:02:16,501
equipment is available for
you to use on board the ship.

30
00:02:16,790 --> 00:02:19,810
For example, computers, the gym equipment.

31
00:02:20,970 --> 00:02:26,510
There would be resource availability,
e.g., you would have a list of staff

32
00:02:26,511 --> 00:02:31,230
available to help you, and there would be
a shop for purchasing items.

33
00:02:32,530 --> 00:02:33,650
Employee capability.

34
00:02:34,370 --> 00:02:36,584
There would be a list of services that can

35
00:02:36,585 --> 00:02:38,910
be provided at the
times that are available.

36
00:02:38,911 --> 00:02:43,550
They would include medical, a gym
instructor, religious services,

37
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:46,970
financial services, dance instruction and
so on.

38
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:52,850
These are the enterprise environmental
factors internal to the cruise liner.

39
00:02:53,390 --> 00:02:54,990
But what about the organization?

40
00:02:56,130 --> 00:03:00,950
One, enterprise environmental factors
internal to an organization.

41
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:06,150
These would include organizational
culture, structure and governance,

42
00:03:06,430 --> 00:03:13,010
as before, e.g., vision, values,
belief, cultural norms, leadership style,

43
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:16,185
hierarchy and authority relationships,

44
00:03:16,186 --> 00:03:20,204
organizational style,
ethics and code of conduct.

45
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:25,570
Then we had geographic distribution of
facilities and resources, easy factory

46
00:03:25,571 --> 00:03:30,470
resources, virtual teams, shared systems,
cloud computing.

47
00:03:31,270 --> 00:03:32,620
There's the infrastructure.

48
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:36,313
Examples include existing
facilities, equipment,

49
00:03:36,314 --> 00:03:40,100
organizational telecommunication
channels, information

50
00:03:40,101 --> 00:03:43,844
technology hardware,
availability and capacity.

51
00:03:44,930 --> 00:03:49,570
Information technology software examples
include scheduling software tools,

52
00:03:50,050 --> 00:03:53,357
configuration management
systems, web interfaces to other

53
00:03:53,358 --> 00:03:56,770
online automated systems
and work authorization systems.

54
00:03:57,510 --> 00:04:02,390
Resource availability examples include
contract and purchasing constraints,

55
00:04:02,890 --> 00:04:06,140
approved providers and subcontractors in
collaboration agreements.

56
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:08,890
And then employee capability.

57
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:13,455
Examples include existing
human resource expertise,

58
00:04:13,456 --> 00:04:16,221
skills, competencies
and specialist knowledge.

59
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:19,698
Let's go to our cruise
liner and consider the

60
00:04:19,699 --> 00:04:22,990
enterprise environmental
factors external to the ship.

61
00:04:23,670 --> 00:04:26,050
There's the marketplace conditions,
e.g.

62
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:30,410
the other competitor shipping companies
that you could have traveled with,

63
00:04:30,870 --> 00:04:33,671
the reputation of the
shipping company that

64
00:04:33,672 --> 00:04:36,904
you chose, the name
and low of the company.

65
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:40,330
Social and cultural influences and issues,
e.g.

66
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:45,040
political climate, codes of
conduct, ethics and perceptions

67
00:04:45,041 --> 00:04:47,581
of the countries that
you're travelling through.

68
00:04:48,250 --> 00:04:52,390
Legal restrictions, for example,
country or local laws or regulations.

69
00:04:53,370 --> 00:04:58,090
For example, you're not allowed to consume
alcohol in some countries and in others,

70
00:04:58,570 --> 00:05:00,375
such as Australia, there are many types of

71
00:05:00,376 --> 00:05:03,570
food that you're not
allowed to bring ashore.

72
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:05,970
Commercial databases e.g.

73
00:05:07,650 --> 00:05:12,650
tables of average ticket prices and
expected facilities, as well as published

74
00:05:12,651 --> 00:05:17,330
data on risk studies, safety reputation of
the various ocean liners, etc.

75
00:05:18,910 --> 00:05:20,060
Academic research, e.g.

76
00:05:20,910 --> 00:05:23,240
online forums and
review sites that rate and

77
00:05:23,241 --> 00:05:26,164
compare shipping
companies and their offerings.

78
00:05:26,730 --> 00:05:29,750
Next is government or industry standards,
e.g.

79
00:05:29,950 --> 00:05:34,810
health and safety laws, employment laws,
such as anti-discrimination, minimum wage,

80
00:05:35,810 --> 00:05:40,710
certifications for crew and equipment,
sea worthness, certification for the ship,

81
00:05:41,170 --> 00:05:44,220
laws relating to the storage, preparation
and serving at food.

82
00:05:45,130 --> 00:05:47,030
And then financial considerations,
e.g.

83
00:05:47,470 --> 00:05:50,129
currency exchange rates, interest rates,

84
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:53,884
inflation rates, tariffs
and geographic location.

85
00:05:54,530 --> 00:05:57,170
Then the physical environment elements,
e.g.

86
00:05:57,630 --> 00:05:59,780
working conditions, weather and
constraints.

87
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:02,570
Okay, so these are the
enterprise environmental

88
00:06:02,571 --> 00:06:04,210
factors external to our cruise line.

89
00:06:04,211 --> 00:06:06,830
but how would we apply those to an
organisation?

90
00:06:07,370 --> 00:06:09,890
So enterprise and
environmental factors external

91
00:06:09,891 --> 00:06:12,710
to the organisation,
marketplace conditions, e.g.

92
00:06:13,370 --> 00:06:16,810
competitors, brand share, recognition and
trademarks.

93
00:06:18,010 --> 00:06:20,930
Social and cultural influences and issues,
e.g.

94
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:24,780
political climate, codes of conduct,
ethics and perceptions.

95
00:06:25,550 --> 00:06:26,750
Then legal restrictions.

96
00:06:26,870 --> 00:06:31,790
Examples include country or local laws and
regulations related to security,

97
00:06:32,070 --> 00:06:35,310
data protection, business conduct,
employment and procurement.

98
00:06:36,570 --> 00:06:38,030
Commercial databases, e.g.

99
00:06:38,370 --> 00:06:42,065
Benchmarking results,
standardized cost-estimating

100
00:06:42,066 --> 00:06:46,510
data, industry risk
information, and risk databases.

101
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:48,950
Academic research, e.g.

102
00:06:49,530 --> 00:06:52,244
industry studies,
publications and benchmarking

103
00:06:52,245 --> 00:06:54,690
results, and government
or industry standards.

104
00:06:55,110 --> 00:07:01,390
Examples include regulatory agency and
standards related to products,

105
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:05,490
production, environment, quality and
workmanship.

106
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:07,910
Financial considerations, e.g.

107
00:07:08,210 --> 00:07:10,473
currency, exchange rates, interest rates,

108
00:07:10,474 --> 00:07:13,784
inflation rates, tariff
and geographic location.

109
00:07:14,270 --> 00:07:18,730
Physical environment elements would be
working conditions, weather and constraints.

110
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:20,300
The same as on boardership.

111
00:07:20,650 --> 00:07:23,700
That brings us to the end of enterprise
environmental factors.

112
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:27,950
I hope by analogy with the cruise ladder
helps you find your sea legs.

113
00:07:28,410 --> 00:07:31,130
Next lesson is 2.2, organizational
process.

114
